readme.config -- The configuration file
=======================================

Services read one configuration file, `ircservices.conf` in the data
directory, together with every file it includes.  It holds the core
settings, the list of modules to load and the settings of each module.
This file describes its syntax and every core setting; the module settings
are in readme.modules, and data/example-ircservices.conf is a complete,
commented example.  Coming from an older version with the line-based
ircservices.conf and modules.conf?  See readme.migration.

1. When the file is read
------------------------

The whole configuration is parsed once, at start-up, before the log file is
opened and before any module is loaded, into a tree kept in memory:

1. the file and everything it includes are read and checked for syntax;
2. the core settings are taken from the tree and checked;
3. modules are loaded in the order of the loadmodule entries; each one
   takes its settings from its `module "<name>"` block(s) in the tree
   before it starts;
4. the tree stays available: modules may look up their settings at any
   time (see readme.api).

Any error in steps 1-3 is reported and Services exit.  On REHASH (OperServ
REHASH or SIGHUP) the same steps are repeated with a new tree; if steps 1
or 2 fail, the new tree is thrown away and nothing changes, and if a
module block has an error, the modules keep the settings they had (see
readme.install, "Reloading the configuration").

2. Syntax
---------

### 2-1. Structure

The file is a sequence of blocks and entries:

    block-name {
        key = value;
        key = value, value, value;
        flag;
    };
    block-name "label" {
        ...
    };
    key = value;
    include "other.conf";

- An **entry** is a name, optionally followed by `=` and one or more values
  separated by commas, and ends with a semicolon.  The `=` may be left out:
  `loadmodule nickserv/main;` and `loadmodule = nickserv/main;` are the
  same.
- A **block** is a name, an optional label, and entries (or other blocks)
  between braces.  The semicolon after the closing brace is optional.
- **include** reads another file in place (section 2-4).

Formally:

    config     = { statement } ;
    statement  = block | entry | include ;
    block      = WORD [ scalar ] "{" { statement } "}" [ ";" ] ;
    entry      = WORD ";"
               | WORD [ "=" ] scalar { "," scalar } ";" ;
    include    = "include" scalar ";" ;
    scalar     = WORD | STRING ;

Block names and entry names are case-insensitive.  Labels (module names)
are case-sensitive.

### 2-2. Words, strings and comments

A **word** is any run of characters other than blanks and

    {  }  ;  =  ,  "  #

Numbers, times, host names, addresses, paths and module names can all be
written as words:

    port = 4400;
    host = 127.0.0.1;
    SendmailPath = /usr/lib/sendmail;
    loadmodule chanserv/access-levels;

Anything else -- text with blanks, or with any of the characters above --
must be a **string**, between double quotes.  Inside a string, `\"` is a
double quote, `\\` a backslash, `\n` a newline and `\t` a tab; any other
backslash sequence stands for the character itself.  A string cannot span
lines.

    description = "Services for \"Example\" Net";
    run_group = "=65534";

The word `include` is reserved; it cannot be used as the name of a block or
entry.

**Comments** run from `#` or `//` to the end of the line, or from `/*` to
`*/` (which may span lines):

    # a comment
    // another one
    /* and a
       longer one */

`#` always starts a comment outside a string, so a value such as a
channel name must be quoted: `"#help"`.

### 2-3. Values

What a value means depends on the setting; the reference below gives the
type of each one.

- **string** -- any word or string.
- **integer** -- a decimal number, or octal with a leading `0`, or
  hexadecimal with a leading `0x`.  Some settings only accept positive
  integers.
- **port** -- an integer between 1 and 65535.
- **time** -- a number of seconds, or a number with a unit: `s` (seconds),
  `m` (minutes), `h` (hours), `d` (days).  Units combine: these all mean
  one day: `86400`, `86400s`, `1440m`, `24h`, `1d`, `23h60m`.
- **seconds** -- a number of seconds that may have a fractional part
  (`0.5`, up to millisecond precision), without unit; used by a few timers.
- **flag** -- the entry alone switches the setting on (`strict_passwords;`).
  A flag may also be given `yes`, `on` or `true` (on) or `no`, `off` or
  `false` (off), in any case: `strict_passwords = no;` is the same as
  leaving the entry out.
- **list** -- several values separated by commas.  A setting that takes a
  list (loadmodule, loadlanguage, reject_email, ...) may also be repeated;
  the values of every entry are taken in order.

Some settings take several values of different types, e.g.
`netbuffer { size = 4194304, 1048576; }` (total, per-connection) or, in a
module block, `NickServName = "NickServ", "Nickname Server";` (nick, real
name).  Trailing values documented as optional may be left out.

When a setting that is not a list is given more than once, the last one
wins.  A block may also be given more than once (in the same file or
across includes); its entries are read in order, as if they had been
written in a single block.

### 2-4. include

    include "filename";

reads `filename` as if its contents were written in place of the include
statement.  include may appear anywhere a block or entry may appear,
including inside a block:

    module "httpd/auth-password" {
        AuthName = "IRC Services";
        include "http-passwords.conf";     # Protect entries, kept apart
    };

A relative filename is relative to the data directory (the current
directory of Services).  Includes may be nested up to 16 levels deep; a
file that includes itself is reported as an error.  A file that cannot be
opened is an error.

3. Errors and warnings
----------------------

Every message names the file and line it is about:

    ircservices.conf:12: syntax error, unexpected '}', expecting ';' or ','
    ircservices.conf:1147: Unterminated string
    ircservices.conf:30: include: unable to open local.conf: No such file or directory
    ircservices.conf:44: list_max: Expected a positive integer for parameter 1
    ircservices.conf: Required directive `numeric' missing from block `serverinfo'
    ircservices.conf:51: Unknown directive `list_maks' in block `options'
    ircservices.conf:60: Warning: unknown block `optoins' ignored

- Syntax errors, missing required settings, values of the wrong type and
  unreadable include files are **errors**: at start-up Services exit, on
  REHASH the previous settings stay in effect.  The parser recovers after a
  syntax error at the next `;`, so several errors are reported at once.
- Unknown entries, unknown top-level blocks, `module` blocks without a
  module name and extra values are **warnings**: the rest of the
  configuration is used.
- A syntax error is reported where the parser noticed it, which may be the
  line after the actual mistake (typically a missing `;`).

4. Reference
------------

The core settings live at the top level and in six blocks: serverinfo,
uplink, files, options, timeouts and netbuffer.  "Required" settings must be
given; the others are optional, and the text says what happens if they are
left out.

### 4-1. Top level

    include "<filename>";
        Read another file here (section 2-4).

    loadmodule <module> [, <module> ...];
        Load the named modules, in order.  May be repeated; the order of
        all loadmodule entries in the file is the load order, and it
        matters: a module must come after the modules it uses (see
        readme.modules for the list and the dependencies).  Removing an
        entry and doing a REHASH unloads the module; adding one loads it.

    loadlanguage "<filename>" [, "<filename>" ...];
        Load replacement text for language strings from the given files
        (relative to the data directory).  The file format is described in
        readme.features, section 3-9.

    module "<module>" { ... };
        The settings of one module; see readme.modules.  A block for a
        module that is not loaded is ignored.

### 4-2. serverinfo -- how Services present themselves

    serverinfo {
        name = "services.example.net";
        description = "Services for IRC Networks";
        numeric = 4094;
        user = "services@example.net";
    };

    name = <server name>;                                  string, required
        The server name Services use on the network.  It must match the
        name of the Connect and UWorld blocks in the uplink's ircd.conf.

    description = <text>;                                  string, required
        The server description, shown in /whois, /links and the like.

    numeric = <0..4095>;                                  integer, required
        The P10 server numeric of Services.  Like the numeric in the
        General block of every ircd.conf, it must not be used by any other
        server on the network.

    user = "<user>@<host>";                                string, required
        The user name and host name of the Services pseudoclients.

name, description and user are only used when Services connect; changing
them takes effect at the next restart.

### 4-3. uplink -- the server Services link to

    uplink {
        host = 127.0.0.1;
        port = 4400;
        password = "linkpass";
        #local_address = 192.0.2.10;
    };

    host = <hostname or address>;                          string, required
    port = <port>;                                           port, required
        The ircu2 server to connect to.  Both may be overridden with the
        -remote command-line option.

    password = <password>;                                 string, required
        The link password, as given in the Connect block of the uplink's
        ircd.conf.

    local_address = <hostname or address> [, <port>];    string [, port]
        The local address (and port) to bind to before connecting, for
        hosts with several addresses.  If not given, the operating system
        chooses; if only the address is given, it chooses the port.

The uplink settings are only used when Services connect; changing them
takes effect at the next restart.

### 4-4. files -- files and process

All filenames are relative to the data directory.

    files {
        log = "ircservices.log";
        pid = "ircservices.pid";
        motd = "ircservices.motd";
        lock = ".lock";
        umask = 077;
    };

    log = <filename>;                                      string, required
        The log file.  May be overridden with the -log command-line option.
        If the name contains `%y`, `%m` or `%d`, they are replaced by the
        current year, month or day, and the log is rotated when the date
        changes.  A change takes effect on REHASH.

    pid = <filename>;                                                string
        The file Services write their process ID to.  If not given, no PID
        file is written.  A change takes effect on REHASH.

    motd = <filename>;                                     string, required
        The Message of the Day file, sent in reply to /MOTD.

    lock = <filename>;                                     string, required
        The data directory lock file, created while the databases are
        being written.  If it already exists when Services want to write
        the databases, they send a warning (WALLOPS) and write nothing.

    umask = <octal mask>;                                   octal integer
        The file creation mask (e.g. 077: files readable by the owner only;
        007: by the group as well) for every file Services create,
        databases included.  If not given, the umask Services were started
        with is kept.

    run_group = <group> | "=<gid>";                                  string
        The group Services run as, by name or, after `=`, by number (quote
        it: `run_group = "=65534";`).  If not given, Services keep the group
        they were started with.

### 4-5. options -- general behaviour

    guest_nick_prefix = <prefix>;                          string, required
        The prefix of the nicknames Services give when they change a user's
        nickname; digits are appended.  ircu2 cannot change a user's
        nickname, but the setting is required anyway.

    list_max = <count>;                          positive integer, required
        The maximum number of items returned by list commands (NickServ
        LIST and LISTEMAIL, OperServ AKILL LIST, ...).

    encryption = <type>;                                             string
        The encryption used for new passwords: the name of an encryption
        module without `encryption/` (`md5`, `unix-crypt`); the module must
        be loaded.  If not given, new passwords are not encrypted.
        Existing passwords are not affected.

    strict_passwords;                                                  flag
        Reject passwords shorter than five characters, in addition to
        passwords equal to the nickname or channel name.

    no_admin_password_check;                                           flag
        Let Services administrators set any password, bypassing the checks.

    bad_pass_limit = <count>;                              positive integer
        KILL a user after this many bad passwords in one session (see
        bad_pass_timeout).  If not given, bad passwords are only logged.

    bad_pass_timeout = <time>;                                         time
        Forget bad passwords after this long without one.  If not given,
        they are forgotten only when the user disconnects.

    bad_pass_warning = <count>;                            positive integer
        Send a WALLOPS after this many bad passwords for a single nickname
        or channel.  If not given, no warning is sent.

    ignore_decay = <seconds>;                                       seconds
    ignore_threshold = <level>;                            positive number
        Flood protection: each user has an "ignore level" that grows with
        the commands they send (and how long those take) and halves every
        ignore_decay seconds; a user whose level exceeds ignore_threshold
        is ignored.  Both must be given for the check to be active
        (example: 5 and 0.1).

    merge_channel_modes = <seconds>;                                seconds
        Delay channel mode changes by this long and send them together.
        Read the warning in data/example-ircservices.conf before using it:
        it lets users keep ops they should not have for that long.

    no_bouncy_modes;                                                   flag
        Do not check for, and stop on, the uplink reversing Services' mode
        changes.

    no_split_recovery;                                                 flag
        Always require users to identify again after a netsplit or a
        Services restart, instead of recognising them by their account
        (see readme.features, section 3-1-2).

    reject_email = <mask> [, <mask> ...];                   list of strings
        E-mail address masks (wildcards allowed) that may not be used to
        register nicknames or channels.  May be repeated.

    timezone = <time zone>;                                          string
        The default time zone for times shown to users (e.g. PST8PDT), at
        most 16 characters.  If not given, the TZ of the environment is
        used.

    log_max_users;                                                     flag
        Log every new maximum of users online.

    enable_getpass;                                                    flag
        Enable the NickServ and ChanServ GETPASS commands.

    wall_admin_privs;                                                  flag
        Send a WALLOPS whenever a Services administrator uses those
        privileges on a nickname or channel.

### 4-6. timeouts -- timers

    timeouts {
        update = 5m;
        warning = 4h;
        read = 3;
        check = 1.0;
        #ping = 30s;
    };

    update = <time>;                                         time, required
        The interval between automatic database saves.  Reset by OperServ
        UPDATE.

    warning = <time>;                                        time, required
        The interval between repeated WALLOPS about program errors.

    check = <seconds>;                                    seconds, required
        The (maximum) interval between checks of the timer list; it sets
        how accurately timed events, such as nickname kills, happen.  At
        most 10; 1 or less is best on a system that can afford it; 0 checks
        on every pass of the main loop.

    read = <seconds>;                                               seconds
        How long Services wait for network data before doing other work
        (such as the timer checks).  If check is smaller than read, the
        timer checks may be further apart than check on a quiet network.

    ping = <time>;                                                     time
        Send a PING to the uplink after this long without traffic.  If not
        given, Services do not send PINGs.

### 4-7. netbuffer -- memory for network buffers

    netbuffer {
        size = 4194304, 1048576;
        limit = 80, 95;
    };

    size = <total> [, <per-connection>];                  positive integers
        The maximum memory, in bytes, used by all network buffers, and by a
        single connection (the total if not given).  Both are rounded down
        to a multiple of the internal buffer size, and must be at least
        twice that size; the per-connection limit may not exceed the total.

    limit = <busy %> [, <ignore %>];                      positive integers
        Thresholds, in percent of size (1 to 99): above the first, Services
        answer PRIVMSGs from non-operators with a "busy" message; above the
        second, they ignore them.  A WALLOPS is sent when a threshold is
        crossed.  The second must not be lower than the first.  Has no
        effect without size.

### 4-8. Summary

    Old directive (ircservices.conf)  New setting
    --------------------------------  --------------------------------------
    RemoteServer host port pass       uplink { host; port; password; }
    LocalAddress host [port]          uplink { local_address; }
    ServerName                        serverinfo { name; }
    ServerDesc                        serverinfo { description; }
    ServerNumeric                     serverinfo { numeric; }
    ServiceUser                       serverinfo { user; }
    LogFilename                       files { log; }
    PIDFilename                       files { pid; }
    MOTDFilename                      files { motd; }
    LockFilename                      files { lock; }
    Umask                             files { umask; }
    RunGroup                          files { run_group; }
    GuestNickPrefix                   options { guest_nick_prefix; }
    ListMax                           options { list_max; }
    EncryptionType                    options { encryption; }
    StrictPasswords                   options { strict_passwords; }
    NoAdminPasswordCheck              options { no_admin_password_check; }
    BadPassLimit                      options { bad_pass_limit; }
    BadPassTimeout                    options { bad_pass_timeout; }
    BadPassWarning                    options { bad_pass_warning; }
    IgnoreDecay                       options { ignore_decay; }
    IgnoreThreshold                   options { ignore_threshold; }
    MergeChannelModes                 options { merge_channel_modes; }
    NoBouncyModes                     options { no_bouncy_modes; }
    NoSplitRecovery                   options { no_split_recovery; }
    RejectEmail                       options { reject_email; }
    DefTimeZone                       options { timezone; }
    LogMaxUsers                       options { log_max_users; }
    EnableGetpass                     options { enable_getpass; }
    WallAdminPrivs                    options { wall_admin_privs; }
    UpdateTimeout                     timeouts { update; }
    WarningTimeout                    timeouts { warning; }
    TimeoutCheck                      timeouts { check; }
    ReadTimeout                       timeouts { read; }
    PingFrequency                     timeouts { ping; }
    NetBufferSize                     netbuffer { size; }
    NetBufferLimit                    netbuffer { limit; }
    LoadModule                        loadmodule
    LoadLanguageText                  loadlanguage
    IncludeFile                       include
    ExpireTimeout                     (removed; it had no effect)

The complete conversion procedure is in readme.migration.
